## Story 003

# The Trembling Mountain

**Status:** Final Manuscript

**Location:** Mont Tremblant, Québec

**Category:** Québec Dark Folklore

---

Long before ski lifts climbed the mountains...

Long before winding highways carried visitors north...

The people who lived among these forests believed the mountain was alive.

Not alive like an animal.

Alive like something ancient.

Something sleeping.

They called it the Trembling Mountain.

Not because it shook often.

But because every so many generations...

It remembered.

The old trappers spoke quietly about it around their campfires.

Loggers laughed whenever the stories were told.

Tourists never heard them at all.

They came to admire the colours.

The lakes.

The mountains.

They never thought to ask why the mountain had its name.

The elders would simply smile.

Then point toward the summit.

"It has trembled before."

Most people assumed they were talking about earthquakes.

They weren't.

They believed something enormous slept beneath the granite.

A guardian.

Older than the forests.

Older than the rivers.

Older even than the people themselves.

It slept peacefully as long as the land remained in balance.

The wolves hunted.

The rivers flowed.

The loons sang.

The forests grew.

Everything had its place.

But when greed outweighed gratitude...

When too much was taken...

When the balance of the wilderness was broken...

The mountain stirred.

Not enough to awaken.

Only enough...

To remind everyone it was still there.

The trembling was never violent.

Sometimes it lasted only a few heartbeats.

A coffee cup rattling on a kitchen table.

A canoe gently rocking against its dock on a perfectly calm morning.

A flock of birds rising from the trees all at once.

The old people would notice.

Then they would quietly stay home for the evening.

Because they believed something else happened whenever the mountain remembered.

The forest listened.

Animals disappeared into silence.

The wind stopped.

Even the insects seemed to forget how to sing.

If you happened to be walking alone beneath the pines...

You might begin to hear footsteps.

Not behind you.

Not ahead of you.

Beneath you.

Slow.

Patient.

As though something impossibly large had shifted deep below the earth.

No one who heard those footsteps ever stayed long enough to discover what made them.

Today...

Millions of people visit Mont Tremblant every year.

They ski.

They hike.

They paddle its lakes.

They stand on the summit taking photographs of one of the most beautiful places in Québec.

Most have never heard the old stories.

And perhaps that's for the best.

Because every so often...

Without warning...

Someone reports feeling the ground gently tremble beneath their feet.

Geologists have their explanations.

The old people simply nod.

Then they look toward the mountain...

And whisper...

"The guardian turned over."

Some say it was only the earth settling.

Others...

Still refuse to whistle in those woods after sunset.